# Launcher handler: production. Serves the frontend build from disk, so nothing about
# apps.tom-sabala.dev depends on Vercel being reachable or on a second public hostname.
#
# The volume is filled by the one-shot `launcher-build` service:
#   docker compose -f gateway/docker-compose.yml run --rm launcher-build
root * /srv/launcher

# Cache policy. Without it Caddy sends no Cache-Control at all and browsers fall back to
# heuristic freshness (a fraction of the file's age), so a launcher deploy can stay
# invisible for days on a machine that already loaded the page — the served HTML is new,
# the screen is not. The HTML is the only mutable document here: it names the hashed asset
# for this build, so revalidating it (304 against the ETag, one cheap round trip) is what
# makes a deploy land. Everything under /assets/ carries a content hash in its filename
# and can never change meaning, so it is immutable for a year.
#
# The two rules are mutually exclusive on purpose: two `header` directives that set the
# same field are applied in wrapping order, so a bare default plus a narrower override
# silently keeps the default. Matchers make the outcome independent of that ordering.
@assets path /assets/*
@document not path /assets/*
header @assets Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
header @document Cache-Control "no-cache"

# `/` is the launcher, not the portfolio SPA.
rewrite / /apps.html

# The build output contains the other two entries as well. They belong on the origin that
# carries the admin session, and serving them here would only create a broken duplicate:
# this origin is absent from the API's CORS_ORIGINS, so every request from them fails.
@portfolio path /index.html /terminal.html
redir @portfolio {$PORTFOLIO_ORIGIN:https://tom-sabala.dev}{uri} 302

# Real file, directory index, then the launcher — which reads the unknown path off the hash
# and renders "No app called X" itself.
try_files {path} {path}/index.html /apps.html
file_server
